course: dictionary
lesson: 7007
title: Saying it in your own words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To paraphrase is to say or write the same thing again in other words. It shows that you have understood it, because you have to know the meaning well enough to say it differently. «Repeating what someone said in your own words is called paraphrasing, and it shows you listened.»
explain: In English and science, paraphrasing is better than copying. If you copy a text word for word, you may not understand it. If you paraphrase, you show you know what it means. A paraphrase keeps the same meaning but uses different words.
explain: Paraphrasing is also useful in everyday life. When you tell your friend what your teacher said, you are paraphrasing. In maths, you can paraphrase a problem by saying it in your own words before you start. This checks that you understand what the question is asking.
fact: To paraphrase means to say the same thing again in other words.
fact: A paraphrase is the new sentence that says the same thing in different words.
fact: A good paraphrase keeps the same meaning as the original.
fact: Paraphrasing shows that you have listened or read carefully.
fact: In maths, paraphrasing a problem is a reading step; you do not change the problem itself.
exercise: «Repeating what someone said in your own words is called paraphrasing, and it shows you listened.» What does paraphrasing mean? | *Saying the same thing again in other words | Copying the words exactly, letter by letter | Writing a long story | Shouting something loudly
exercise: Which sentence is a paraphrase of "The match was cancelled because of rain"? | The match was cancelled because of rain | *Rain meant the game did not go ahead | Ella likes football | The sun was out and the match was on
exercise: In science you read "Plants need light, water and air to grow." Which is a paraphrase? | *Plants grow when they get light, water and air | Plants need light, water and air to grow | Light is very bright | Plants are green
test: Which is the best paraphrase of "Be home by six"? | Six is my favourite number | Stay out late | Be home by six | *Get back before six o'clock
test: Why do we paraphrase in English? | To make the text longer | To hide the meaning | *To show we understand the text in our own words | To copy it so we don't have to think
exam: Jayden is asked to say a maths word problem in his own words before he starts. Why? | To make the problem longer | To skip the problem | To change the answer | *To check he understands what the question is asking
